Subject: the desktop's shell is called Raye where people read its name

The owner's photos: an error from the Start menu headed "Plasma Desktop
Workspace", and a file copy started from the desktop with that name and the
Plasma logo.

Both come from one place. Measured on 2026-11-07 against this source:

    libnotificationmanager/notification.cpp:349-350, 387-388
        serviceName = service->name();
        applicationIconName = service->icon();
        ...
        if ((isDefaultEvent || applicationName.isEmpty()) && !serviceName.isEmpty())
            applicationName = serviceName;
    libnotificationmanager/jobsmodel_p.cpp:228-229   the same for a job

The notification server looks the sender up by its desktop entry, here
org.kde.plasmashell, and shows that file's Name and Icon. KIO dialogs take the
window title from the application's display name, which main.cpp sets.

The file carries its own translations, 60-odd Name[xx] lines. They go too: one
left behind is the old name in that language, and this is not a word anyone
needs translated. "Raye" is what a person sees in French and in English.

The plugin name stays plasmashell. D-Bus, the session's service and every
config file key on it, and no person reads it.

Reason: the name lives in a file the plasma-workspace package owns, so a
config file, a script or a look-and-feel package cannot change it. A copy under
/usr/share/applications from another package conflicts with this one, and
XDG_DATA_DIRS order would decide which is read, for every program at once. A
second implementation of "what is the sender called" outside the source is
what org.raye.clock has to do for other senders; for the shell itself the
name can simply be right.

